      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61944,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A subset of tumours arise from endometriosis {11293158; 29241974}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:02:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61945,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>Ovarian adenosarcomas are typically unilateral and large, ranging from uniformly solid to solid and cystic. Cysts are often small. Polypoid fronds may be seen within cysts or on the ovarian surface. Expansile fleshy areas may indicate sarcomatous overgrowth. Haemorrhage and necrosis may be seen {12360039}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:55:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61946,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Low-power examination reveals evenly distributed glands and cysts within a predominant stromal component. The glands are often elongated, compressed, and branching, imparting a phyllodes-like appearance. The stroma may form polypoid projections within cysts, and it is often more cellular adjacent to the epithelial component (cuffing). The cells lining the glands and cysts are cytologically bland and exhibit tubal, endometrioid, mucinous, or squamous differentiation; sometimes there is an admixture of cell types. Rarely, the glands exhibit features of atypical hyperplasia or low-grade endometrioid adenocarcinoma. The stroma (which may be decidualized) resembles neoplastic endometrial stroma and may show fibroblastic or smooth muscle differentiation or be myxoid. Cytological atypia in the stromal cells is usually mild to moderate but in rare instances is severe; mitotic activity is variable, occasionally being conspicuous {12360039; 20179434}. Sex cord&ndash;like differentiation (sometimes extensive, more often granulosa-like) {25656930; 26535983}, foamy histiocytes, and multinucleated stromal cells may be seen. Heterologous elements, including skeletal muscle, cartilage, and adipocytic elements, may be present {22449231; 12360039}. Sarcomatous overgrowth is defined as areas of pure sarcoma without glands, occupying at least 25% of the tumour; the pure sarcoma is most commonly high-grade&nbsp;&ndash; either undifferentiated sarcoma or rhabdomyosarcoma {2535774; 12360039; 20179434; 19483627; 12558756}.</p>\r\n<p>The stromal component is typically positive for CD10, WT1, ER, and PR. Areas of high-grade sarcomatous overgrowth are usually negative for these markers; desmin, myogenin, and MYOD1 may be positive in areas of skeletal muscle differentiation {18469708; 16051333}. Sex cord&ndash;like areas may be positive for inhibin and other sex cord markers.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:44:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
